%% both_boot.glp --- BOTH mini-apps under one super-app, on two smartphones
%% (SGSG paper, Section 7, and Theorem 6.5): under the boot harness, one
%% isolate per person, each with its own key pair, running the social graph of
%% graph/core as its super-app and hosting GC's app (programs/apps/gc) and
%% CSSN's app (programs/cssn), each compiled from its contract, at the same
%% time.  Each person trusts the bundled compiler's key, reads both certified
%% artefacts from graph/core with load_file/2 and installs both, each with its
%% own person channel.  alice cold-calls bob,
%% they befriend across the isolates, alice invites him to both mini-apps, bob
%% consents to both, and each handshake opens a root channel of that platform's
%% own protocol; each super-app activates both entries by run/3, each with its
%% own conversations.  Then both platforms run at once over the one friendship:
%% alice and bob issue, swap and pay under GC's contract, as play_coins of
%% programs/apps/gc, and befriend under CSSN's.  The program is graph/core,
%% named as :boot's second argument:
%%   :artefact programs/gsc/checker programs/social/graph/core
%%   :artefact programs/apps/gc programs/social/graph/core
%%   :artefact programs/cssn programs/social/graph/core
%%   :boot programs/social/both_boot.glp programs/social/graph/core
%% Expected on the persons' screens, among their lines: [alice] view(alice,
%% [coin(bob), knows(bob), knows(bob), identity, person], [bob], [], _) and
%% view(alice, [friend(bob), knows(bob), identity, person], [bob], [], _);
%% [bob] gave(swap(alice, bob, alice, 2, bob, 3), yes) and view(bob,
%% [friend(alice), knows(alice), identity, person], [alice], [], _).

procedure boot.
boot :-
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@alice,
    say(alice, both_invite, [bob], ch(_?, _))@alice,
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@bob,
    say(bob, both_consent, [alice], ch(_?, _))@bob.

%% Every agent's initial goal is agent(ch(_?,_),ch(_?,_)), the person channel
%% first and the network channel second (GLP-Spec maglp.tex, Definition
%% "Multiagent GLP"), which the program, graph/core, answers: a boot defines
%% no agent/2, and the person goal's first act names the entry the program
%% starts (IGLP 0e1c9e0, Implementation Notes, "Boot").  The person scripted
%% at each agent, say/4, run on its person channel in the person's place,
%% tells the agent the entry, sg_init/4, with its own key, its mode and the
%% keys of the peers it knows, its ground arguments, alice and bob being the
%% atoms that stand for the keys in a simulation ("An agent learns the keys of
%% its peers from its person"; Remark "Agent Names"), and reads nothing.
SayAct ::= sg_init(Key, Constant, List(Key)).
SayIn  ::= [SayAct | SayEnd].
SayEnd ::= [].

exported procedure say(Key?, Constant?, List(Key)?, Channel(Stream(Constant), SayIn)?).
say(P, M, Qs, ch(_, [sg_init(P?, M?, Qs?)])).
